Original commit body from 3f16899bac782721f5483036f03fadd63682bef5:

hypergumbo has no user-level configuration of any kind: the rust-analyzer
gate accepts only HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER and --backend (which cli.py
normalises into that same env var), and there is no $XDG_CONFIG_HOME reader
anywhere. The only way to make the choice durable is exporting the env var
from a shell profile — which is GLOBAL, so it opts every Rust repo the user
ever analyses into build.rs execution, contradicting the standing "never on
untrusted repos" rule the gate exists to enforce.

Eight rulings. The load-bearing ones: two stores (preferences in
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME, trust grants in $XDG_STATE_HOME, because ~/.config is
designed to be synced and a synced trust grant is the footgun again);
a trust grant is never a config key; project-local config may not set
security-gated keys (inverting ADR-0016 §35 / ADR-0017 §370's
"project-local outranks built-in", which is right for catalogs and exactly
wrong for trust); and backend opt-in is two concepts with two homes, keyed
off a per-backend executes_analysed_code bit so the next backend cannot land
in the wrong store by omission.

Reuses two ADR-0013 patterns: out-of-tree $XDG_* state, and a human-owned
agent-unwritable file — which matters more here than it does for the
tracker, since a trust store an autonomous agent can write is not a trust
store. Departs from ADR-0013 on keying: repo-fingerprint deliberately shares
across checkouts, which is right for a cache and wrong for trust.

OQ1 labels the weakest ruling (advisory rather than revoking content hash —
a behavioural judgement, not a measurement). OQ5 records that no user
reported this; the trigger was an authored fixture and a code read.

Trigger: WI-sobig. Related: ADR-0012, ADR-0016, ADR-0017, ADR-0022 (a
different sense of "configuration surface" — disambiguated in the text),
WI-nanom, WI-jivim, WI-gojum.

